input InputWhenRepeat {
  count: Int
  hourly: [WRHourlyRange!]
  weekly: [WRWeeklyRange!]
  monthly: [WRMonthlyRange!]
  yearly: [WRYearlyRange!]
  every: WREveryRange
  timeZone: String
}

input InputWhenRule {
  start: TimeUTC
  end: TimeUTC
  repeat: InputWhenRepeat
}

type WhenRepeat {
  count: Int
  hourly: [WRHourlyRange!]
  weekly: [WRWeeklyRange!]
  monthly: [WRMonthlyRange!]
  yearly: [WRYearlyRange!]
  every: WREveryRange
  timeZone: String
}

type WhenRule {
  start: TimeUTC
  end: TimeUTC
  repeat: WhenRepeat
}

"""
The range that represents the valid every XXX statement,

Examples:
- 30s
- 5m
- 4h
- 12d
- 2w
- 6m
"""
scalar WREveryRange

"""
WRHourlyRange represents the valid hours of the day, 0-23, and minutes 0-59. Each value can be a single hour or a range of hours, separated by a dash.

Examples:

00:00
16:00-17:00
16:32-17:21
"""
scalar WRHourlyRange

"""
The range that represents the valid days of the month, 1-31, end is for each end of the month
Each value can be a single day or a range of days, separated by a dash.
For example, "1-31" represents the first 31 days of the month, while "end" represents the end of any month, e.g., 28th of February, 30th of April, 31st of May, etc.

Examples:

- 1-31
- 3
- end
"""
scalar WRMonthlyRange

"""
Examples

mon
tue
wed
thu
fri
sat
sun

mon-wed
sat-sun
"""
scalar WRWeeklyRange

"""
The range that represents the valid days of the year, 1-366, end is for each end of the year
Each value can be a single day or a range of days, separated by a dash.
For example, "4-6" represents the 4th through 6th days of the year, while "end" represents the
end of any year, e.g. the 365th day of a common year or the 366th day of a leap year.

Examples:

- 3
- 4-6
- end
"""
scalar WRYearlyRange